Z||000005||fierospeeder||11-23-2001||04:46 AM||||woohoo  no more soccer moms driving SUVs<P><BR>this was on the news recently.  The US can be less dependent of middle eastern oil if we stopped buying SUVs.<P>Then they were interviewing the people who were buying the SUVs at the car lot.  90% of them were soccer moms that drove over the guard rails at the gas pumps.<P>Its fine if the gas prices are cheap, but eventually it will go back up again.  Because our supply will be short.  If everyone was driving motorcycles.  Same with peoples driving habits of wasting gas.  Gas would be 50cents if we used it wisely.  Figure some cents for tax etc..<P>Its like a tickle me elmo doll, there wasn't enough during xmas and the price went up.  And there were stupid people paying 1000 bucks and now when there is 50000000000000 elmos its only 2 bucks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||11-23-2001||06:51 AM||||I think the low gas prices is only going to be temporary.  When I started driving (four years ago) gas was as low as it is now.  Within two years gas prices had risen to over 1.50 a gallon everywhere, and now they've fallen back down.<P>Also, supply and demand doesn't fit the gas prices very well.  Gas prices are effected more by the government than anything else.  Look how much fuel costs in Europe!!!  They're closer and use just as much, probably less because they have many more economy cars than us.  So, fuel costs should go down right?  Well, I think they're at about 3-4 times the cost that we pay.  So much for that model.<P>Oh, another side note, don't you think it's odd that gas prices plummet after this national crisis?  It's no coincidence; you know that gas isn't becoming any cheaper for us to obtain, but the government is tweaking something somewhere (or everywhere <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fformula88||11-23-2001||08:22 AM||||SUV's only took the place of station wagons, and in fact are nothing more than gas sucking, high riding station wagons.  I do not care for them either, except I will admit they have their place for some people.  <P>Gas prices fluctuate based on the market, and since the Sept 11 attacks the demand has gone down.  Not necessarily for gasoline as much but other industries have used a lot less fuel, namely the airline industry.  It didn't operate for a couple days at all, and still isn't operating at the levels it was.  That has put the demand on oil down, which is reflected in prices at the pump.<P>Prices had gone up due to a shortage of refining capability which put supplies down.  A couple major British refineries were closed for maintenance, as well as some US facilities including the big one in California that blew up a couple years ago.<P>I dunno whether the gov't has anything to do with it.  I initially suspected Bush, but the more I have read about it the more it sounds like actual economic forces were driving the prices.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pontiaddict||11-23-2001||08:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierospeeder:<BR><B><P>Its fine if the gas prices are cheap, but eventually it will go back up again.  Because our supply will be short.  If everyone was driving motorcycles.  Same with peoples driving habits of wasting gas.  Gas would be 50cents if we used it wisely.  Figure some cents for tax etc..<P>Its like a tickle me elmo doll, there wasn't enough during xmas and the price went up.  And there were stupid people paying 1000 bucks and now when there is 50000000000000 elmos its only 2 bucks.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Except that if everyone drove motorcycles they would just cut back the production to create a demand (or do something drastic like boycott foreign oil or something) then charge you 15 bucks a gallon for gas. The only thing you'd be able to drive from then on was a motorcyle becasue that's the only thing you could afford to put gas in.||||reg||1
Z||000009||maryjane||11-23-2001||10:56 AM||||See this picture, with the lake in the background? That is as close as I can get with the Fiero. I fish a LOT, and it's why I have the Cherokee. It gets around 20mpg in town and 27mpg on the highway. Not great but it's ok. I could get a pickup, but then would have to either take all my fishing stuff & tools out of it when it is parked somewhere other than my home or put a camper shell on it. I hate having to crawl into one of those little shells to get something out.<BR>Nobody seems to complain about the heavy fuel-hungry Surburbans, they really suck the gas down. I like having something with some room in it, that I have access to my stuff without going around to the shell to get it.<BR>For those of us that need them, suvs are the way to go.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero_24_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Philphine||11-23-2001||12:02 PM||||can't knock suv's really. i've only had one truck, a 2dr s10 blazer. not an excursion but not a samuri either. it was way too practical once i got it. getting rid of it but i really want another. we get just enough bad weather to make you glad to have one in winter, and packing anything very big in a two seater (if you can get it on/in the car) is way more fun than you want to have twice. even if it's not my everyday car i'd always like to have one.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||11-23-2001||05:14 PM||||We used to have a Blazer, it was a good truck.. I've driven my bosses Jeep Grand Cherokee before, and it's quite nice.<P>I like the smaller SUVs (I can't imagine ever having a need for a suburban etc) but I don't need one.. too bad that doesn't stop most of the people who buy them.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>(ever seen what happens when a careless driver in a suburban hits a parked cavalier at 85mph? not much left of the cavalier, nor anyone inside it.)<P>p.s. while I agree about the content of the commercial... after the first couple of sconds I just wanted to punch that "chug-a-lug" guy...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||12
